Agile & Scrum, Exercises. Part 1

1. 
Having worked in waterfall I can confirm it works fine if the people and team know what they are doing. However, it goes wrong when one is applying this methodology
to something that requires incremental changes or reviews, like, let's say a wedding. When planning it, one constantly needs to adjust things and review them along the way.
Building a building, there, a design should be set and not changed unless some earth-shatterign event happens. In software development, like apps or games, it is good to
have an incremental approach.

2.
Individuals and interactions over processes and tools - when following a scrum by the book, at one point we had no room for work on the side ( if it ever came ). However,
when it did, instead of coming together as a team and solving the problem, we got told to stick to the rules and continue. The outcome was not great.

Working software over comprehensive documentation - documenting is important, however, it must be a fine combination of how much one spends documenting over writing code. 
A good example we had in a team: "I can sit in jira and make and document tasks for 7 hours a day, or I can write code for 7 hours". A good scrum master will coach the team
to find the combination of right time allocation.

